# Kubernetes Lab

Hands-on Kubernetes lab focused on cluster deployment, networking, application hosting, troubleshooting, and infrastructure automation.

The environment is built using Ubuntu Linux and MicroK8s and is used to develop practical experience with Kubernetes administration and containerized infrastructure services.

## Lab Environment

The lab currently consists of a multi-node Ubuntu environment running MicroK8s.

Technologies include:

- Ubuntu Server
- Kubernetes / MicroK8s
- containerd
- Calico networking
- Helm
- NetBox
- PostgreSQL
- Valkey
- Linux networking
- kubectl

The environment is used for hands-on testing rather than production workloads.

## Kubernetes Cluster

The lab covers:

- MicroK8s installation and upgrades
- Multi-node cluster configuration
- Node joining and removal
- Kubernetes namespaces
- Pods
- Deployments
- Services
- Persistent storage
- Cluster networking
- Container runtime troubleshooting
- Application health checks

Example administrative commands:

```bash
microk8s status
microk8s kubectl get nodes
microk8s kubectl get pods -A
microk8s kubectl get services -A
microk8s kubectl describe pod <pod-name>
microk8s kubectl logs <pod-name>
```

## Kubernetes Networking

A major focus of this lab is understanding Kubernetes networking from a network engineer's perspective.

Topics include:

- Pod addressing
- Service addressing
- ClusterIP services
- Calico CNI
- Inter-node communication
- DNS
- Service discovery
- Application ports
- Network troubleshooting
- Connectivity between Kubernetes and the physical network

This provides practical experience connecting traditional network engineering concepts with containerized infrastructure.

## NetBox on Kubernetes

NetBox is deployed in the Kubernetes lab using Helm.

The deployment includes:

- NetBox
- PostgreSQL
- Valkey
- NetBox workers
- Kubernetes services
- Persistent application components

The environment is being used to explore NetBox as a source of truth for network automation.

## Troubleshooting

The lab is intentionally used to reproduce and troubleshoot real Kubernetes problems.

Examples include:

- Pods failing startup probes
- Container connection failures
- Cluster node communication problems
- Services not reaching application pods
- Helm deployment issues
- Dependency startup ordering
- Kubernetes networking problems

Troubleshooting tools include:

```bash
kubectl get pods
kubectl describe pod
kubectl logs
kubectl get events
kubectl get endpoints
kubectl get services
```

## Integration with Network Automation

Future development will integrate this Kubernetes environment with the separate `network-automation` project.

Planned integrations include:

- NetBox REST API
- Automated network inventory
- Device synchronization
- Configuration compliance
- Network source-of-truth validation
- Containerized network automation tools

## Roadmap

Planned lab development includes:

- NetBox API automation
- Ingress configuration
- Persistent storage testing
- Kubernetes network policies
- Application monitoring
- Prometheus and Grafana
- High-availability testing
- Containerizing Python network automation tools
- CI/CD experimentation

## Security

This repository contains lab-built and sanitized examples only.

No employer, customer, production credentials, configurations, API keys, certificates, or sensitive network information are published.


## Cluster Health Script

A Bash-based cluster health utility is included under:

```text
scripts/cluster-health.sh
```

The script performs a basic operational health assessment of the MicroK8s environment, including:

- MicroK8s readiness
- Kubernetes node state
- Pod health
- Services
- Endpoints
- Persistent volume claims
- Recent Kubernetes events

Run it from a MicroK8s node:

```bash
chmod +x scripts/cluster-health.sh
./scripts/cluster-health.sh
```

Example sanitized output:

```text
------------------------------------------------------------
 Node Readiness Check
------------------------------------------------------------
PASS: All Kubernetes nodes are Ready.

------------------------------------------------------------
 Pod Health Check
------------------------------------------------------------
PASS: No unhealthy pods detected.

============================================================
 Health Check Complete
============================================================
Overall status: PASS
```
